counterfactuals.output:
The invention is an enhanced electronic chart display and information system for collision avoidance. It combines chart, heading, speed, and vessel-tracking data—such as AIS, radar, and GPS—to present nearby traffic relative to the operator’s own vessel.

For each approaching vessel, the interface graphically displays:

- its current position, heading, speed, and projected track;
- the predicted point and time at which its track will cross the own vessel’s track;
- whether it will pass on the port or starboard side;
- closest-point-of-approach and time-to-closest-approach information;
- color-coded or audible warnings when a collision-risk threshold is reached; and
- the applicable navigation-rule obligation, such as whether the own vessel is the give-way or stand-on vessel.

The display may use track lines, crossing-point symbols, countdown labels, shaded danger zones, and recommended maneuver arrows so that the navigator can understand the encounter at a glance. By converting complex motion and collision calculations into an intuitive graphical presentation, the invention reduces interpretation time, helps prevent misjudgment, and supports timely maneuvers consistent with collision-avoidance regulations.

A ship navigation system may comprise a radar subsystem, an Electronic Chart Display and Information System (ECDIS), a positioning subsystem, a processing unit, and a display and alarm interface. The radar subsystem is configured to detect one or more target vessels and to generate target data representing, for each detected vessel, at least a measured range and bearing relative to an own vessel. The positioning subsystem is configured to provide own-vessel navigation data, including geographic position, heading, speed, and optionally rate of turn.

The processing unit is configured to transform the radar target data from a vessel-relative coordinate frame into a geographic coordinate frame used by the ECDIS. The transformed target data is correlated with successive radar observations and, where available, Automatic Identification System data to establish a target track comprising an estimated target position, course, speed, and associated confidence value. The target track is registered with electronic chart information supplied by the ECDIS, thereby enabling each tracked vessel to be displayed at a corresponding geographic location on an electronic navigational chart.

For each tracked vessel, the processing unit predicts a future own-vessel trajectory and a future target-vessel trajectory over a predetermined prediction interval. The predicted trajectories may be determined according to a constant-course and constant-speed model, a rate-of-turn model, a route-based model, or a combination thereof. The processing unit calculates an encounter parameter comprising at least one of a closest point of approach (CPA), a time to closest point of approach (TCPA), a predicted separation distance at a selected future time, or a probability that a protected region associated with the own vessel will be entered by the target vessel.

The processing unit compares the encounter parameter with one or more configurable threshold values. In one embodiment, a warning condition is established when the predicted CPA is less than a threshold distance and the TCPA falls within a threshold time interval. The applicable threshold may be fixed or may be dynamically selected in accordance with vessel speed, vessel dimensions, navigational area, charted hazards, traffic density, weather conditions, or a user-defined safety policy.

Upon establishment of the warning condition, the display interface graphically presents the predicted encounter as an overlay on the ECDIS chart. The overlay may include predicted vessel tracks, time-indexed position markers, velocity vectors, a closest-point-of-approach location, a predicted safety-domain intersection, and a textual indication of the calculated CPA and TCPA. A target vessel associated with the warning condition may be visually distinguished by color, line style, flashing, highlighting, or a warning symbol. The system may additionally generate an audible, visual, or haptic alarm, with alarm priority being determined according to the predicted distance, time to encounter, relative velocity, and confidence of the target track.

In exemplary claim-style language:

> A navigation assistance system for an own vessel, comprising:  
> a radar interface configured to receive radar observations of a target vessel;  
> an electronic chart interface configured to receive electronic navigational chart data;  
> a navigation data interface configured to receive position and motion data of the own vessel;  
> at least one processor configured to:  
> (i) derive, from the radar observations, a geographic position and motion state of the target vessel;  
> (ii) register the geographic position of the target vessel with the electronic navigational chart data;  
> (iii) predict respective trajectories of the own vessel and the target vessel;  
> (iv) determine a predicted separation parameter between the respective trajectories;  
> (v) compare the predicted separation parameter with a threshold distance; and  
> (vi) responsive to determining that the predicted separation parameter satisfies a warning criterion, cause an encounter indication and a warning notification to be presented; and  
> a display configured to present the electronic navigational chart together with the target vessel, the predicted trajectories, and the encounter indication.

The system may further account for radar measurement uncertainty, chart-position uncertainty, and target maneuver uncertainty by defining an uncertainty region around each predicted target position. A warning may then be issued when the uncertainty region intersects a safety domain surrounding the own vessel, thereby providing a probabilistic or confidence-weighted encounter warning rather than relying solely upon a single deterministic trajectory.
